proof:
The support geometry and central-pair calculations from `cd6a1446e7ce3997` concern the unchanged objects (1) and the restriction \(\phi(\mathbf0,y)=a\|y\|_\infty\). We verify those subclaims for the present real matrix below. The mass comparison uses the real-shift statement of `f55232eea7862d8c`, without applying the integer-matrix mass theorem of `cd6a1446e7ce3997` to new hypotheses.

Every point of \(R\) can be written as \(t v-(1-t)\mathbf1_r\), with \(0\le t\le1\) and \(v\in[0,h_x]^r\). Its coordinates are at least \(t-1\ge-1\) and at most \((h_x+1)t-1\le h_x\). A negative integer coordinate must equal \(-1\), and the lower bound then forces \(t=0\). Thus the integer points of \(R\) are exactly \(-\mathbf1_r\) and \(\{0,\ldots,h_x\}^r\).

Every point of \(P\) has the form \((x,y)=(\lambda v,(1-\lambda)v')\), where \(v\in R\), \(v'\in Q\), and \(0\le\lambda\le1\). Its first projection belongs to \(R\), since \(\mathbf0\in R\). If an integer first projection is \(-\mathbf1_r\), then \(x_i\ge-\lambda\) forces \(\lambda=1\) and \(y=\mathbf0\). Otherwise its first projection is nonnegative, and the bounds \(x_i\le\lambda h_x\) and \(\|y\|_\infty\le(1-\lambda)h_y\) imply the displayed inequality in (3). Conversely, for such a nonnegative \(x\), set \(\lambda=\|x\|_\infty/h_x\). For \(0<\lambda<1\), the points \(x/\lambda\in[0,h_x]^r\) and \(y/(1-\lambda)\in Q\) provide the required representation in \(P\). For \(\lambda=0\), \(x=\mathbf0\) and \(y\in Q\); for \(\lambda=1\), \(y=\mathbf0\) and \(x\in[0,h_x]^r\). This proves (3).

The finite integer vertices generating \(P\) all belong to \(D\), so \(P=\operatorname{conv}D\). The origin is the strictly positive barycenter, with coefficients \(1/(r+1)\), of the affinely independent points \(-\mathbf1_r,\mathbf e_1,\ldots,\mathbf e_r\) in \(R\). Therefore \(\mathbf0\in\operatorname{int}R\). Since also \(\mathbf0\in\operatorname{int}Q\) and \((R/2)\times(Q/2)\subseteq P\), the origin is interior to \(P\). This also proves \(\dim P=n\).

The function \(\phi\) is convex on \(P\): the first term is a convex function under projection, and the second is a nonnegative multiple of a norm composed with a real linear map. This argument imposes no arithmetic condition on \(B\). Define \(f=e^{-\phi}\) on \(P\) and \(f=0\) outside \(P\). It is finite-valued. Convexity of \(P\) and \(\phi\) proves log-concavity when both endpoints lie in \(P\); when either endpoint is outside \(P\), the right side of the log-concavity inequality is zero. By (3), \(f\) agrees with every lattice value in (2), including every zero. Thus the table has the required global extension. Convexity gives \(G_\lambda\ge0\) for every barycentric support, and \(\phi_{\mathbf0}=0\) gives \(w_{\mathbf0}=1\).

Apply `f55232eea7862d8c` with its \(s=s\), \(h=h_y\), \(Q=Q\), and \(a=a\). Its shift \(v\) is any real vector here, and its central partition value \(Z_Q(a;0)\) is exactly \(Z_{\mathrm{cube}}\). Its conclusion gives, for every \(v\in\mathbb R^s\),

\[
\sum_{y\in Q\cap\mathbb Z^s}e^{-a\|y-v\|_\infty}
\le2\gamma^s Z_{\mathrm{cube}}.
\tag{7}
\]

This includes \(a=0\) and shifts outside the cube.

On \(\{-\mathbf1_r\}\cup\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^r\), retain the auxiliary first-block law \(\rho_x=2^{-(r+1)}2^{-(x_1+\cdots+x_r)}\) from `cd6a1446e7ce3997`. Its mass at the negative apex is \(1/2\), and its total mass on the nonnegative orthant is \(2^{-(r+1)}(\sum_{j\ge0}2^{-j})^r=1/2\). Hence \(\sum_x\rho_x=1\). Define, solely for comparison on the original \(D\),

\[
q_{(x,y)}=
\frac{\rho_x e^{-a\|y-Bx\|_\infty}}
{2\gamma^s Z_{\mathrm{cube}}}.
\tag{8}
\]

These \(q_{\mathbf I}\) are auxiliary numbers, distinct from the continuous dimension \(q\) in the later approximation construction. Every original first projection is in the domain of \(\rho\), and every original second projection belongs to \(Q\cap\mathbb Z^s\). Equation (7) applies with \(v=Bx\) for each such \(x\), including \(x=-\mathbf1_r\). Extending the sum only in this auxiliary comparison gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
0<\sum_{(x,y)\in D}q_{(x,y)}
&\le\sum_{x\in\{-\mathbf1_r\}\cup\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^r}
\rho_x
\frac{\sum_{y\in Q\cap\mathbb Z^s}e^{-a\|y-Bx\|_\infty}}
{2\gamma^s Z_{\mathrm{cube}}}\\
&\le\sum_x\rho_x=1.
\end{aligned}
\tag{9}
\]

Positivity follows, for example, from the original origin site. Thus \((q_{\mathbf I})_{\mathbf I\in D}\) is a positive subprobability on \(D\).

Let \(p_{\mathbf I}=w_{\mathbf I}/W\) be the original probability law. Jensen's inequality for the logarithm gives \(\sum_Dp_{\mathbf I}\log(q_{\mathbf I}/p_{\mathbf I})\le\log\sum_Dq_{\mathbf I}\le0\). Substitution of (2) and (8) cancels the entire coupled norm cost and gives the exact identity

\[
\begin{aligned}
0
&\le\sum_Dp_{(x,y)}\log\frac{p_{(x,y)}}{q_{(x,y)}}\\
&=-\sum_Dp_{(x,y)}V(x)-\log W
 +(r+1)\log2+\log(2\gamma^s Z_{\mathrm{cube}})
 +(\log2)\sum_Dp_{(x,y)}\sum_{i=1}^r x_i\\
&=-\sum_Dp_{(x,y)}V(x)-\log W
 +(r+1)\log2+\log(2\gamma^s Z_{\mathrm{cube}}).
\end{aligned}
\tag{10}
\]

The last equality uses precisely the first-block coordinates of the stipulated original mean. At the same original law, convexity of \(V\) gives \(\sum_Dp_{(x,y)}V(x)\ge V(\sum_Dp_{(x,y)}x)=V(\mathbf0)=0\). Equation (10) proves \(W\le2^{r+1}\,2\gamma^s Z_{\mathrm{cube}}\). No comparison-law weights have entered either the mean assumption or the hitting capacities.

At \(x=\mathbf0\), the table is exactly \(w_{(\mathbf0,y)}=e^{-a\|y\|_\infty}\) because \(B\mathbf0=\mathbf0\). The singleton \(\{\mathbf0\}\) has gap zero. Each unordered pair \(\{(\mathbf0,y),(\mathbf0,-y)\}\) with \(y\ne\mathbf0\) is an affinely independent barycentric support with coefficients \(1/2,1/2\), gap \(a\|y\|_\infty\), and equal endpoint weights. These unordered pairs are mutually disjoint and avoid the singleton. Every hitting set must therefore pay for the singleton and one full endpoint from every pair with gap at most \(L\). Hence

\[
m_{\log}(\mathbf0,L;w)
\ge1+\frac12
\sum_{\substack{y\in Q\cap\mathbb Z^s,\ y\ne\mathbf0\\a\|y\|_\infty\le L}}
e^{-a\|y\|_\infty}.
\tag{11}
\]

We verify the central-tail estimate used in `cd6a1446e7ce3997` on this identical central cube. Define \(\eta(y)_j=\operatorname{sgn}(y_j)\lfloor|y_j|/2\rfloor\), with \(\operatorname{sgn}(0)=0\). Its image stays in \(Q\cap\mathbb Z^s\), and \(\|\eta(y)\|_\infty\le\|y\|_\infty/2\). A zero coordinate of the image has at most the preimages \(-1,0,1\), and a nonzero coordinate has at most two preimages. Thus each image vector has at most \(3^s\) preimages, including after restriction to the finite cube, and

\[
\sum_{y\in Q\cap\mathbb Z^s}e^{-(a/2)\|y\|_\infty}
\le\sum_{y\in Q\cap\mathbb Z^s}e^{-a\|\eta(y)\|_\infty}
\le3^s Z_{\mathrm{cube}}.
\tag{12}
\]

For \(a>0\), the strict tail beyond the weak cutoff \(4s\) consequently satisfies

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{\substack{y\in Q\cap\mathbb Z^s\\a\|y\|_\infty>4s}}
e^{-a\|y\|_\infty}
&\le e^{-2s}\sum_{y\in Q\cap\mathbb Z^s}e^{-(a/2)\|y\|_\infty}\\
&\le(3e^{-2})^sZ_{\mathrm{cube}}
<\frac12Z_{\mathrm{cube}}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{13}
\]

Indeed \(e^2>1+2+2+8/6>6\), so \(3e^{-2}<1/2\), and \(s\ge1\). For \(a=0\) the tail is empty. In all cases the eligible central-cube mass is at least \(Z_{\mathrm{cube}}/2\). The right side of (11) is half that eligible mass plus \(1/2\), which proves \(m_{\log}(\mathbf0,4s;w)\ge Z_{\mathrm{cube}}/4\).

With the proved total-mass bound and \(n=r+s\), this yields

\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{m_{\log}(\mathbf0,4s;w)}{1+4s}
&\ge\frac{Z_{\mathrm{cube}}}{4(1+4s)}\\
&\ge\frac{W}{2^n}
\frac{2^{s-3}}{2\gamma^s(1+4s)}
\ge\frac{W}{48\,2^n}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{14}
\]

The last step is exactly the absorption inequality of `f55232eea7862d8c`, with the same integer \(s\) and the same \(\gamma\). Explicitly, its middle factor equals \(3^{s/2}/[16(1+4s)]\) and is at least \(1/48\) for every \(s\ge1\). The finite-cutoff attainment statement of `488f0a583886b5fc` applies to our globally extendable table and shows that the cutoff maximum exists; it is at least its value at \(4s\). This proves (4).

For the stationary irrational family, fix \(r=2\), \(s=1\), \(B=(\sqrt2,-\sqrt2)\), \(h_x,h_y\ge2\), and \(a>0\). Use the same finite-moment construction parameters \(u,c_-,\delta\) as in `cd6a1446e7ce3997`, now defined with the actual present costs:

\[
\begin{aligned}
u&=\frac1{2h_x(h_x+1)^2(2h_y+1)},\\
c_-&=\sum_{\substack{(x,y)\in D\\x\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^2}}
x_1u^{x_1+x_2}e^{-a|y-\sqrt2(x_1-x_2)|},
&\delta&=-\log(c_-u^2),\\
V(x)&=-(\log u)(x_1+x_2)
+\delta\max\{0,-x_1,-x_2\}\quad(x\in R).
\end{aligned}
\tag{15}
\]

Here \(0<u<1\), and the site \((\mathbf e_1,0)\in D\) contributes \(u e^{-a\sqrt2}>0\) to \(c_-\). In every nonzero summand, \(x_1\le h_x\), \(x_1+x_2\ge1\), and the exponential is at most one. The number of summands is at most \((h_x+1)^2(2h_y+1)\), so \(0<c_-\le h_x(h_x+1)^2(2h_y+1)u=1/2\). Consequently \(c_-u^2<1\) and \(\delta>0\). Formula (15) is a finite convex function on all of \(\mathbb R^2\), since it is an affine function plus a positive multiple of a maximum of affine functions. It vanishes at the origin.

At every nonnegative projected site its original weight is precisely \(u^{x_1+x_2}e^{-a|y-\sqrt2(x_1-x_2)|}\). At the unique negative projected site \((-1,-1,0)\), the coupling cost is zero and \(V(-1,-1)=2\log u+\delta=-\log c_-\), so its original weight is exactly \(c_-\).

The involution \((x_1,x_2,y)\mapsto(x_2,x_1,-y)\) preserves the nonnegative part of (3). It also preserves the just-displayed original weights: the exponent \(x_1+x_2\) is unchanged, and the expression inside the absolute value changes sign. The first moment of this part is therefore \((c_-,c_-,0)\), using the definition of \(c_-\) for the first coordinate and the involution for the other two. The negative apex contributes \((-c_-,-c_-,0)\). The full original moment is exactly zero, so dividing by \(W>0\) proves the required stationarity.

To check coupling on the actual lattice support, the four sites \((\mathbf0,0)\), \((\mathbf0,1)\), \((\mathbf e_1,0)\), and \((\mathbf e_1,1)\) belong to \(D\). For the last site this uses \(1/h_x+1/h_y\le1\), including equality when both side lengths equal two. Since \(|1-\sqrt2|=\sqrt2-1\), their exact potential differences are

\[
\phi_{(\mathbf e_1,1)}-\phi_{(\mathbf e_1,0)}=-a,
\qquad
\phi_{(\mathbf0,1)}-\phi_{(\mathbf0,0)}=a.
\tag{16}
\]

These cannot both equal \(T(1)-T(0)\) when \(a>0\), so no representation \(\phi_{(x,y)}=U(x)+T(y)\) exists on \(D\). This proves the stated original-table example with irrational coupling.

For the exact-table actual-body conclusion, let \(C\) satisfy (5) in its actual continuous dimension \(d\). Its positive-volume support is exactly \(D\), its total is \(W_C=\alpha W\), and its target fiber has positive volume \(w^C_{\mathbf0}=\alpha\). Its original normalized law is \(w^C/W_C=w/W\), hence has the stipulated original mean. The normalized potential of `488f0a583886b5fc` for this actual table is
\(\log(w^C_{\max}/w^C_{\mathbf I})=\log(\alpha w_{\max}/(\alpha w_{\mathbf I}))=\phi_{\mathbf I}-\min_D\phi\).
Thus logarithmic eligibility is identical for the two tables, and every hitting set has its original capacity multiplied by \(\alpha\). For every cutoff,

\[
m_{\log}(\mathbf0,L;w^C)
=\alpha m_{\log}(\mathbf0,L;w).
\tag{17}
\]

For \(E\in\mathcal E_{\mathbf0}\), with its unique positive coefficients \(\lambda_{E,\mathbf I}\), the actual root cost of `7fa5a28fe1ac6893` is

\[
\begin{aligned}
L_E^C
&=\log\frac{w^C_{\mathbf0}}
{\left(\sum_{\mathbf I\in E}\lambda_{E,\mathbf I}(w^C_{\mathbf I})^{1/d}\right)^d}\\
&=-d\log\left(\sum_{\mathbf I\in E}\lambda_{E,\mathbf I}e^{-\phi_{\mathbf I}/d}\right)\\
&\le\sum_{\mathbf I\in E}\lambda_{E,\mathbf I}\phi_{\mathbf I}
=G_E.
\end{aligned}
\tag{18}
\]

The common scale \(\alpha\) cancels exactly, and the inequality follows from convexity of the exponential, or weighted arithmetic-geometric mean. These actual root costs are nonnegative by `7fa5a28fe1ac6893`. Therefore every logarithmically eligible independent support is root eligible at the same cutoff. The root family contains the logarithmic family and uses exactly the same capacities \(w^C\). Every set hitting the root family must hit the logarithmic family, which proves
\(m_d(\mathbf0,L;w^C)\ge m_{\log}(\mathbf0,L;w^C)\).
This is the pointwise comparison of `488f0a583886b5fc`, derived here with the actual dimension and normalization, and with all equal-cost cases retained.

Apply the original-fiber inequality (4) of `7fa5a28fe1ac6893` at \(\mathbf J=\mathbf0\) and \(L=4s\), with the exact object map in the statement. Equations (14), (17), and (18) give

\[
\begin{aligned}
\max_{z\in C_{\mathbf0}}h_S((\mathbf0,z))
&\ge\frac{m_d(\mathbf0,4s;w^C)}{6(1+4s)}\\
&\ge\frac{\alpha m_{\log}(\mathbf0,4s;w)}{6(1+4s)}
\ge\frac{\alpha W}{288\,2^n}
=\frac{W_C}{288\,2^n}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{19}
\]

The same accepted fact proves attainment of this maximum on the original fiber. Select an attaining \(z^\star\in C_{\mathbf0}\) before any halfspace is specified. Its depth is the infimum of the original mixed volumes over all containing closed ambient halfspaces, so every such halfspace has the bound (6). This includes vertical halfspaces and all boundary mass. The original support, original weights, and actual continuous dimension remain those in (1), (2), and (5) throughout.

For the actual-body approximation conclusion, the table \(w\) is globally extendable, \(\psi\) is normalized and convex-admissible, and \(P\) has full dimension \(n\), as already proved. The roof statement of `488f0a583886b5fc` therefore applies with the map in the statement. It makes \(\widehat\psi\) continuous, convex, piecewise affine, equal to \(\psi\) on \(D\), and valued in \([0,\beta]\). Its full-dimensional construction has no transverse thickening in the present case, and is exactly the displayed \(C^{(q)}\). Equivalently, its upper cube side length is a positive concave piecewise-affine function on the compact full-dimensional \(P\). This proves convexity, compactness, polyhedrality, and full dimension of \(C^{(q)}\). At each integer \(\mathbf I\in D\) the fiber is the cube \([0,w_{\max}^{1/q}(1-\psi_{\mathbf I}/q)]^q\). Each integer \(\mathbf I\notin D\) lies outside \(P\), by (3), so that fiber is empty. Thus the asserted complete table is exact and its target fiber is positive.

Equations (5)-(8) of `488f0a583886b5fc` give the following comparisons on these precise capacities and in their actual dimension \(q\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
0&\le\varepsilon_q\le\frac{\beta^2}{2(q-\beta)},
&e^{-\varepsilon_q}W&\le W^{(q)}\le W,\\
t_q(L)&=\min\{L+\varepsilon_q,L/\kappa_q\},
&m_q(\mathbf0,t_q(L);w^{(q)})
&\ge e^{-\varepsilon_q}m_{\log}(\mathbf0,L;w)
\quad(L\ge0).
\end{aligned}
\tag{20}
\]

Here \(m_q\) is the actual root-hitting mass in continuous dimension \(q\), using all original capacities of \(C^{(q)}\). The eligible-support comparison is at the same integer target \(\mathbf0\); it follows from the accepted root costs of these constructed cubes and does not invoke any stationarity assertion about \(w^{(q)}\). More explicitly, for every \(E\in\mathcal E_{\mathbf0}\) its root cost is
\(q\log[(q-\psi_{\mathbf0})/(q-\sum_E\lambda_{E,\mathbf I}\psi_{\mathbf I})]\).
The accepted inequality bounds this by both \(G_E+\varepsilon_q\) and \(G_E/\kappa_q\). Thus a logarithmically eligible support at \(L\) remains root eligible at \(t_q(L)\), while every new capacity is at least \(e^{-\varepsilon_q}\) times its original scalar capacity. These are exactly the two comparisons underlying (20).

For every \(L\ge0\), the definition of \(t_q\) gives

\[
1+t_q(L)\le(1+\varepsilon_q)(1+L),
\qquad
1+t_q(L)\le\frac{1+L}{\kappa_q},
\qquad
\frac{1+L}{1+t_q(L)}
\ge\max\left\{\kappa_q,\frac1{1+\varepsilon_q}\right\}.
\tag{21}
\]

For the second inequality, \(0<\kappa_q\le1\). Apply `7fa5a28fe1ac6893` to the actual body \(C^{(q)}\), with \(d=q\), target \(\mathbf0\), and cutoff \(t_q(4s)\). Combining its original-fiber inequality with (14), (20), and (21) yields

\[
\begin{aligned}
\max_{z\in C^{(q)}_{\mathbf0}}h_{S^{(q)}}((\mathbf0,z))
&\ge\frac{m_q(\mathbf0,t_q(4s);w^{(q)})}{6(1+t_q(4s))}\\
&\ge e^{-\varepsilon_q}
\max\left\{\kappa_q,\frac1{1+\varepsilon_q}\right\}
\frac{m_{\log}(\mathbf0,4s;w)}{6(1+4s)}\\
&\ge e^{-\varepsilon_q}
\max\left\{\kappa_q,\frac1{1+\varepsilon_q}\right\}
\frac{W}{288\,2^n}\\
&\ge e^{-\varepsilon_q}
\max\left\{\kappa_q,\frac1{1+\varepsilon_q}\right\}
\frac{W^{(q)}}{288\,2^n}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{22}
\]

All changes of capacities in this argument are the explicit original volumes \(w^{(q)}\) of the new body. The scalar stationarity assumption was used only in (10), before this construction. Attainment in `7fa5a28fe1ac6893` supplies one \(z_q^\star\) in the fixed original fiber of \(C^{(q)}\); choosing it before \(H\) gives the asserted inequality for every containing closed halfspace and its full original mixed volume. Finally \(\kappa_q\to1\) and \(\varepsilon_q\to0\) follow from (20) for the fixed finite amplitude \(\beta\). This proves the coefficient limit and supplies actual approximating bodies also for every irrational stationary scalar example above.
